WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.840
Você gostou de dar um modelo AI, você sabe, custom superpowers usando o seu próprio Java code?

00:05.840 --> 00:08.720
Bem, nesse explicador, isso é exatamente o que vamos fazer.

00:08.720 --> 00:11.600
Vamos construir nosso primeiro server de tools de AI da área,

00:11.600 --> 00:14.160
e vamos ver como simplesmente o spring AI faz.

00:14.880 --> 00:17.280
Você sabe, essa quarta realmente nega, não é?

00:17.280 --> 00:21.200
Conhecer como construir essas brujas entre o seu código e um modelo AI.

00:21.200 --> 00:23.360
Não é apenas um projeto um pouco divertido.

00:23.360 --> 00:28.000
É rapidamente se tornando uma skill fundamental para qualquer desenvolver que quer ficar ahead.

00:28.080 --> 00:30.880
E hoje, você está tomando um grande passo nessa direção.

00:31.680 --> 00:32.800
E os números?

00:32.800 --> 00:34.480
Bem, eles falam para eles mesmos.

00:34.480 --> 00:36.960
Isso não é um futuro muito longe que nós estamos falando sobre.

00:36.960 --> 00:38.480
Está acontecendo agora.

00:38.480 --> 00:43.920
Um 92% da fortuna de 500 empresas já está no jogo de AI,

00:43.920 --> 00:46.960
e três de cada quatro desenvolvedores estão lá com eles.

00:46.960 --> 00:48.560
Então, vamos dar certeza de que você é um deles.

00:49.280 --> 00:50.560
Então, aqui é nosso plano de jogo.

00:50.560 --> 00:53.360
Vamos começar com o que um server de mcp mesmo é.

00:53.360 --> 00:57.200
Então, vamos rolar as nossas brujas e construir um, conectar-se a um real AI,

00:57.280 --> 01:00.880
e finalmente, vamos olhar o mundo grande da possibilidade que isso abre.

01:00.880 --> 01:01.600
Vamos entrar.

01:02.400 --> 01:03.920
Ok, primeiro coisa primeiro.

01:03.920 --> 01:06.800
O que exatamente é esse server que estamos aboutando construir?

01:06.800 --> 01:08.880
A forma mais fácil para pensar sobre isso é isso.

01:08.880 --> 01:12.000
É uma forma de dar o seu modelo AI favorito, brand new abilities,

01:12.000 --> 01:15.040
custom superpowers que você criou com o seu próprio código.

01:15.760 --> 01:20.160
Agora, não deixe o nome model context protocolo intimidar você.

01:20.160 --> 01:22.240
A palavra quente aqui é standard.

01:22.240 --> 01:23.440
Isso é tudo.

01:23.440 --> 01:26.320
Think of mcp as a universal adapter.

01:26.320 --> 01:30.000
It's just a common language that lets all these different AI models,

01:30.000 --> 01:35.120
like clod or gemini, securely plug into and use external tools,

01:35.120 --> 01:36.800
like the Java code we're about to write.

01:37.520 --> 01:39.520
And really, that's all there is to it.

01:39.520 --> 01:41.200
There's no dark magic here.

01:41.200 --> 01:44.960
It is just the agreed upon standard way for an AI to call a function

01:44.960 --> 01:46.560
that you wrote in Java.

01:46.560 --> 01:49.120
You could build a tool that check your company's inventory,

01:49.120 --> 01:52.800
or maybe access a special API or perform some custom math.

01:52.800 --> 01:55.840
M.C.P.S. is simply the bridge that lets the AI use it.

01:56.640 --> 01:58.160
All right, enough theory.

01:58.160 --> 02:01.120
Let's get our hands dirty and actually build this thing.

02:01.120 --> 02:03.040
We're going to walk through this step by step,

02:03.040 --> 02:05.520
and I think you'll be pretty amazed at just how easy Springs

02:05.520 --> 02:06.560
makes this whole process.

02:07.200 --> 02:11.920
It all starts where so many great projects do start.spring.io.

02:12.480 --> 02:15.360
All you have to do is choose your usual project settings,

02:15.360 --> 02:18.800
and then for the dependencies, you only need one thing.

02:18.800 --> 02:22.160
Just search for model context protocol server, add it,

02:22.240 --> 02:23.520
and that's literally it.

02:23.520 --> 02:25.200
Hit generate, and you're good to go.

02:26.000 --> 02:28.800
And this visual just drives the point home.

02:28.800 --> 02:30.400
Look how simple that is.

02:30.400 --> 02:32.080
You don't need some complex setup

02:32.080 --> 02:34.240
with a dozen different dependencies.

02:34.240 --> 02:37.120
Spring AI has neatly packaged everything we need

02:37.120 --> 02:39.360
into this one single starter.

02:39.360 --> 02:42.320
With just a couple of clicks, our project skeleton is ready.

02:43.120 --> 02:44.560
Okay, now for the code.

02:44.560 --> 02:47.600
Which is, believe it or not, also incredibly simple.

02:47.600 --> 02:50.480
First, you just write a plain old Java service class.

02:50.560 --> 02:53.120
Put the methods in there that you want the AI to be able to call.

02:53.680 --> 02:57.120
Second, for each method, you add the at tool annotation.

02:57.120 --> 02:59.200
And this is absolutely crucial.

02:59.200 --> 03:02.240
You give it a clear natural language description.

03:02.240 --> 03:04.480
This description is how the AI reasons

03:04.480 --> 03:06.400
about when it should use your tool.

03:06.400 --> 03:09.040
Finally, you just register these tools with Spring

03:09.040 --> 03:10.480
by creating a simple bean.

03:11.280 --> 03:14.000
Now listen up, because this is a huge pro tip

03:14.000 --> 03:15.840
that will save you a major headache.

03:15.840 --> 03:17.440
Some clients, like Claude Desktop,

03:17.440 --> 03:19.200
talk to our server using something called

03:19.200 --> 03:21.040
standard input slash output.

03:21.040 --> 03:22.240
For that to work cleanly,

03:22.240 --> 03:25.520
you have to turn off that cool looking spring boot startup banner.

03:25.520 --> 03:28.160
Just add spring.main.banner-mode

03:28.160 --> 03:30.160
is off to your configuration file.

03:30.160 --> 03:31.840
If you don't, the client can get confused

03:31.840 --> 03:34.080
by the banner art and the connection will fail.

03:34.080 --> 03:35.680
Seriously, don't forget this line.

03:36.640 --> 03:38.240
Okay, we've built the project.

03:38.240 --> 03:39.680
We've written the code.

03:39.680 --> 03:41.440
Now for the magic moment.

03:41.440 --> 03:43.760
Let's connect our server to a real AI

03:43.760 --> 03:46.080
and watch this whole thing come to life.

03:46.080 --> 03:47.120
This is the fun part.

03:47.920 --> 03:49.920
The very last piece of the puzzle

03:49.920 --> 03:51.520
is to tell the AI client,

03:51.520 --> 03:53.600
in this case we're using Claude Desktop,

03:53.600 --> 03:55.520
how to actually run our server.

03:55.520 --> 03:57.360
You'll find a little configuration file,

03:57.360 --> 03:58.960
usually a JavaScript file,

03:58.960 --> 04:00.800
where you define custom servers.

04:00.800 --> 04:02.800
You just add a new entry for your tool

04:02.800 --> 04:05.600
and give it the command to run the .jar file we built,

04:05.600 --> 04:08.720
which is just java-jar your file.jar.

04:09.360 --> 04:12.400
And after you save that config and restart your client,

04:12.400 --> 04:13.680
this is the payoff.

04:13.680 --> 04:15.760
You open up the tool settings in the AI

04:15.760 --> 04:18.320
and boom, there they are.

04:18.320 --> 04:19.760
Your custom tool names

04:19.760 --> 04:22.160
with the descriptions you wrote listed right there.

04:22.800 --> 04:24.880
In this example, DV GetCourse

04:24.880 --> 04:27.120
is now a real usable capability

04:27.120 --> 04:28.640
that the AI knows about.

04:28.640 --> 04:29.520
How cool is that?

04:30.160 --> 04:32.640
And this is where it all clicks into place.

04:32.640 --> 04:34.640
You can now have a normal conversation

04:34.640 --> 04:35.440
with the AI

04:35.440 --> 04:38.160
and ask it something it had no way of knowing before.

04:38.160 --> 04:40.160
When you ask about Dan Vega's courses,

04:40.160 --> 04:41.680
the AI's reasoning engine will go,

04:41.680 --> 04:43.760
hey, wait a minute, I have a tool for that.

04:43.760 --> 04:46.240
And it will execute your Java code to get the answer.

04:46.960 --> 04:50.640
So we've basically just built the hello world of AI tools.

04:50.640 --> 04:53.200
But this is just the very, very beginning.

04:53.200 --> 04:54.400
Let's zoom out for a second

04:54.400 --> 04:57.120
and just look at the incredible universe of possibilities

04:57.120 --> 04:58.880
that this one skill opens up.

04:59.440 --> 05:00.640
What you've learned here today

05:00.640 --> 05:03.440
is a foundational building block for so much more.

05:03.440 --> 05:05.440
You could take this and build a full chat app

05:05.440 --> 05:06.800
with conversational memory.

05:06.800 --> 05:08.560
Or what about a powerful search engine

05:08.560 --> 05:10.480
that understands the meaning of your documents

05:10.480 --> 05:12.960
by using a vector database like PG Vector?

05:13.040 --> 05:14.800
You could even dive into multimodal apps

05:14.800 --> 05:17.120
where your AI can understand images, audio.

05:17.120 --> 05:18.480
The sky is truly the limit.

05:19.040 --> 05:20.480
And this brings us to what I think

05:20.480 --> 05:22.960
is the most important takeaway from all of this.

05:22.960 --> 05:25.280
You're no longer just a consumer of AI.

05:25.280 --> 05:27.600
You are now a builder of AI tools.

05:28.560 --> 05:30.640
I love this quote from Sam Altman

05:30.640 --> 05:32.320
because it connects what we just did,

05:32.320 --> 05:34.080
this technical exercise,

05:34.080 --> 05:36.640
to a fundamental part of being human.

05:36.640 --> 05:38.080
We build tools.

05:38.080 --> 05:39.840
You didn't just write some code.

05:39.840 --> 05:41.760
You built a tool that literally extends

05:41.760 --> 05:43.600
the capabilities of an AI,

05:43.600 --> 05:46.000
one of the most powerful technologies of our time.

05:46.720 --> 05:48.560
And that's the bottom line.

05:48.560 --> 05:50.720
You now have the foundational skills

05:50.720 --> 05:52.320
and you have the incredible tools

05:52.320 --> 05:54.000
from the spring ecosystem

05:54.000 --> 05:56.000
to start building your own unique,

05:56.000 --> 05:57.760
powerful AI integrations.

05:58.560 --> 06:00.720
So, I'll leave you with this one question.

06:00.720 --> 06:02.960
You successfully built your very first bridge

06:02.960 --> 06:04.800
between your code and an AI.

06:04.800 --> 06:07.760
You've given it a new command, a new superpower.

06:07.760 --> 06:09.520
The only question left is,

06:09.520 --> 06:11.040
where are you going to command it to go next?

